Strong negative responses mark Zhao/Zheng spellings, several romanized
Chinese/Korean syllables, the lone character 赤 (red), and data/material
vocabulary. Split UTF-8 characters are joined; the unfinished byte prefix
shared by 赵, 资, and 赤 responds before the character is complete.
Some split kanji give positive pulses, annotated below. Diff hunk minuses,
repeated middle dots, and document-initial C++ comment lines are separate
negative punctuation senses. These rules are lexical, not general semantic
classifiers, and leave most small background activations unexplained.
